WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.860
The mystery of the Hulk's sizzled right arm
in Avengers: Endgame has finally been answered.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:09.300
The reason the Green Goliath was injured so
badly using the Infinity Stones while, if

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.660
you'll recall, Thanos was not is simple.

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:15.960
According to co-director Joe Russo, it was
a purposeful choice to continue highlighting

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:19.949
the magnitude of the Mad Titan's strength,
as well as to display how potent the Infinity

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:21.180
Stones are when used.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.039
In the film, well-adjusted and emotionally
stable Professor Hulk, a merging of the brains

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.100
and mild temperament of Bruce Banner with
the green, super-strong physique of the Hulk,

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.320
wielded the Infinity Stones to bring back
the lives lost in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.000
Of course, his act of service didn't come
without a heavy penalty: Hulk experienced

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.760
substantial cosmic charring up his entire
right arm and part of his neck.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.100
By the end of the movie, the arm was rendered
pretty much useless.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.280
Since he's presented as one of the most powerful
characters in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.300
it came as a surprise to the audience — and
even his fellow Avengers — that Hulk took

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.600
such a substantial hit using the Infinity
Stones — especially since he was deemed

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.510
most likely to survive the impact of using
Tony Stark's Infinity Gauntlet 2.0 to channel

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.220
their power.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.260
"You saw what those stones did to Thanos.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.840
They almost killed him.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.100
None of you could survive."

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.680
"How do we know you will?"

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:12.880
"We don't."

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.160
Fans will recall that in Infinity War, Thanos
used the stones to snap half of all life in

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.630
the universe out of existence, with relatively
little injury to himself — though his Infinity

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.900
Gauntlet was heavily damaged in that first
usage.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.280
It was only when he used the Stones again
— to destroy them, this time — that it

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.530
really took a toll, as he emerged from the
experience with a noticeable hobble and some

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.320
pretty bad damage to his Gauntlet arm.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.751
Russo explained that it was important for
the filmmakers to revisit how the Hulk, for

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.840
all his incredible power, paled compared to
Thanos.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:43.940
He explained:

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.660
"We know how powerful Thanos is by his quick
defeat of the Hulk in Infinity War, so this

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.000
is an incredibly painful experience, to put
on the gauntlet that controls the universe.

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:57.340
It permanently damages the most powerful beings
and some of the most powerful creatures in

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.800
these movies, and very few people can actually
put that on and survive."

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.860
Marvel Studios' head of visual development
Ryan Meinerding also explained that in earlier

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:10.029
development, they considered giving Hulk a
more substantial injury in the form of physically

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:14.499
shrivelling his arm, but ultimately abandoned
the idea due to technical problems presented

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.020
in later fight sequences.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.240
Meinerding revealed:

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.939
"When Hulk brings everybody back with a snap
using the Stark gauntlet, they wanted Hulk

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.840
to have paid a severe price for doing it.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.960
The idea of his arm being shriveled and messed
up was where they were going with it initially,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:28.460
and...

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.299
I pitched that basically when his arm gets
shriveled, he just rips his sleeve off and

00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:36.519
turns it into a sling to allow him to fight
later on in the film and not have that asymmetry

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.120
be something that was too odd.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.460
In the end, they worked it out fine in the
movie without that concept."

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.980
Endgame makes a big deal of exacting cost;
it's fair to say that Thor and Hulk are diametrically

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.200
opposed examples of the best and worst ways
to cope with the fallout of Infinity War …

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.800
"Oh my god!

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:54.320
Oh my god!"

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.980
"It's so good to see you!"

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.600
"Come here you little rascal!"

00:02:59.100 --> 00:02:59.940
"No, no, I'm good!

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:00.760
I'm good!"

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.779
… so in lending gravity to Hulk's decision
to use the Stark gauntlet, it would have made

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.020
no sense to have him make angst-ridden soliloquies
like Thor.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.180
Hulk's grasp on the situation, however, did
not suddenly make him more physically able

00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:15.849
than he was in Infinity War, and it would
have cheapened both movies to suddenly grace

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:17.400
him with Thanos-level strength.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.880
One of the main themes of Avengers: Endgame
is sacrifice.

00:03:20.889 --> 00:03:25.180
The film demonstrates over and over again
that the only way to be victorious over "inevitable"

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:30.340
defeat is through cooperation, teamwork, and
costly actions that exact a terrible personal

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:30.840
price.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.799
The Avengers' strategy for reversing the Snap
was an incredibly complex one, and every action

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:36.960
had consequences.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.340
It just so happened that some of those consequences
were more painful than others.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.460
Endgame's doling out of terrible prices for
each small victory was poetic and effective.

00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:50.689
It illustrated that even for Earth's Mightiest
Heroes, reversing an action as drastic as

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:55.740
the Snap would truly require them to be willing
to do say it with us whatever it takes.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.299
In a universe where anything can happen and
be undone as is convenient, what makes us

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:04.819
care is characterization, and characters who
actually have to suffer before achieving success.

00:04:04.819 --> 00:04:09.589
In a film with stakes as high as Endgame,
that suffering comes swift and hard even if

00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:10.700
you're the Incredible Hulk.

